# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mi CV con IA — environment variables
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. NEVER commit real values.
# Variables WITHOUT the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix are server-only and must never reach
# the browser (Azure OpenAI key, Supabase service role key, Amplitude server key).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- App ---
# ONLINE-ONLY: this app cannot run offline. AI_PROVIDER=mock is rejected at
# startup (see ONLINE_ONLY in lib/env.ts) — you MUST use "azure", which calls the
# real Azure OpenAI API and requires the key + base URL below.
AI_PROVIDER=azure
NODE_ENV=development

# --- Azure OpenAI (server-only) ---
AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
# The **v1** endpoint of your Azure OpenAI resource. The trailing /openai/v1 is
# required: that surface speaks plain OpenAI wire format, so no api-version query
# parameter is needed. Validated at startup in lib/env.ts.
AZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://<your-resource>.cognitiveservices.azure.com/openai/v1
# The DEPLOYMENT name on that resource (usually named after the model it serves),
# used for adaptive questioning + resume generation. The *-codex models are served
# only on the Responses API, which is what lib/ai/azure-openai-provider.ts calls.
# For a lower-cost deployment, gpt-5-mini / gpt-5-nano already have rates
# configured in lib/ai/pricing.ts.
AZURE_OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.3-codex

# --- Supabase ---
# Public values are safe to expose to the browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Service role key — SERVER-ONLY. Bypasses RLS. Never expose to the browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Employer accounts (the talent directory's login) ---
# The directory at /empleadores is gated: an employer registers with their name,
# company and email, and must confirm that address before they can see a single
# candidate. See AUTH_PRODUCTION_SETUP.md for the full runbook.
#
# AUTHENTICATION IS SUPABASE'S, END TO END. Account, password hashing, sessions,
# refresh, the confirmation email, the recovery email and the tokens inside them
# are all GoTrue's. This app issues no credential of its own. Delivery is Resend,
# configured as Supabase Custom SMTP in the dashboard — NOT called from this
# codebase.
#
# There are therefore NO Resend variables here, and there should not be. A
# RESEND_API_KEY in this file would mean the app was sending authentication mail
# itself, which is the thing this design deliberately does not do. The SMTP
# credentials live in the Supabase dashboard.

# --- Public base URL ---
# Used to build the redirect handed to Supabase (emailRedirectTo / redirectTo),
# which is what the STOCK email templates come back to. Optional: it is normally
# derived from the request headers, which is what lets preview deployments and
# both brand domains each send links back to themselves. Set it when a proxy
# rewrites the host, or when previews must send links to the real domain.
#
# !! Whatever this resolves to MUST also be on Supabase's allow-list:
# !! Authentication -> URL Configuration -> Redirect URLs. A destination that is
# !! not listed is silently replaced by the project's Site URL, and the employer
# !! lands somewhere else with no error to see.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# --- Supabase dashboard settings this code REQUIRES ---
# (Nothing in this block is an environment variable. It is configured in the
#  dashboard, and the app does not work correctly without it.)
#
#   1. Authentication -> Providers -> Email -> "Confirm email" = ON
#      This is the REVERSE of what an earlier version of this file said, and the
#      reverse of what migration 0013 required. With it ON, signUp returns no
#      session and signInWithPassword refuses an unconfirmed address, so the
#      confirmation is enforced by GoTrue rather than only by our own gate.
#
#   2. Authentication -> URL Configuration
#      Site URL = your production origin. Redirect URLs must include, for every
#      environment you use:
#          https://<host>/auth/callback
#          https://<host>/auth/confirm
#          http://localhost:3000/auth/callback   (local only)
#          http://localhost:3000/auth/confirm    (local only)
#
#   3. Authentication -> Emails -> Templates
#      Replace the stock templates with the token_hash versions in
#      docs/auth-email-templates.md. The stock ones use a PKCE link that only
#      works in the browser that started the flow — the wrong browser for most of
#      this audience, who sign up on a shop computer and read mail on a phone.
#
#   4. Project Settings -> Authentication -> SMTP Settings
#      Enable Custom SMTP and point it at Resend. Without it, Supabase's built-in
#      sender is capped at a handful of messages an hour and only delivers to
#      addresses inside your Supabase organisation — messages to everyone else
#      are dropped with no error anywhere.
#
#   5. Authentication -> Providers -> Email -> Password Requirements =
#      "Lowercase, uppercase letters, digits and symbols", minimum length 10,
#      to match lib/employers/policy.ts.

# --- Mail identity (NOT the authentication path) ---
# Authentication email is sent by Supabase, so these do NOT configure it — that
# sender is set in Supabase's SMTP Settings. What remains here is the seam for
# the one message this product may still send itself: the talent directory's
# manage link, which goes to a job seeker who has no account and which Supabase
# will therefore never send. No transport implements it today.
#
# ONE address serves BOTH brands. Rumbo Latino is an Aprende product, not a
# separate company, so there is no rumbolatino.com mailbox of its own. A display
# name here would put "Aprende Institute" in the From line of an email whose body
# says Rumbo Latino, which recipients read as phishing — so the address is config
# and the NAME comes from the resolved brand. lib/env.ts rejects a display name.
MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=no-reply@auth.rumbolatino.com
#
# Where replies go. A no-reply address means a small business owner who answers
# the email gets silence, and this audience answers emails.
MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# --- Analytics (server-only) ---
# Leave blank to disable analytics (events become no-ops).
AMPLITUDE_API_KEY=


# --- PDF renderer ---
# Which browser renders the résumé PDF (lib/resume/pdf-generator.ts):
#   auto        (default) serverless on Vercel/Lambda, full puppeteer elsewhere
#   local       full `puppeteer` — a devDependency, needs its ~300 MB Chromium
#   serverless  `puppeteer-core` + `@sparticuz/chromium` (a Lambda-sized build)
#
# You should not need to set this: `auto` detects Vercel/Lambda from the runtime,
# not from NODE_ENV, so a self-hosted production server still uses the full
# browser. Set it explicitly only to reproduce one path on the other.
# PDF_RENDERER=auto
#
# Optional on Vercel: PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD=true keeps the build from ever
# fetching the ~300 MB Chromium the serverless path does not use.

# --- Persistence mode ---
# ONLINE-ONLY: PERSISTENCE=memory is rejected at startup (see ONLINE_ONLY in
# lib/env.ts). You MUST use "supabase" and configure the URL/keys above.
PERSISTENCE=supabase

# --- Branding ---
# Two marketing brands from one build: "rumbo-latino" and "aprende". The brand is
# chosen per request from the request host.
# HOW TO SWITCH: docs/switching-brands.md
#
# Brand used when the host matches NO brand — preview deploys, localhost, and any
# domain not listed in a brand config. Unset => rumbo-latino.
#
#   !! This does NOT override a host a brand already claims. On rumbolatino.com
#   !! the host wins and this is ignored; use BRAND_HOST_OVERRIDES there.
#
# A value that is not a registered brand id fails at startup rather than silently
# serving the fallback. Ids are lowercase and hyphenated: rumbo-latino | aprende.
DEFAULT_BRAND=rumbo-latino
#
# Point specific hosts at a brand. Comma-separated host=brandId. Consulted BEFORE
# each brand's own hosts list, so this is how you flip a claimed domain.
# BRAND_HOST_OVERRIDES=rumbolatino.com=aprende,www.rumbolatino.com=aprende
#
# For local work, append ?brand=aprende (or ?brand=rumbo-latino) to any URL; the
# choice sticks for the session. ?brand=auto clears it.

# --- Resume guidelines ---
# The AI follows the style/format guidelines in `resume-guidelines.md` (project
# root) when generating and analyzing resumes. Edit that file to change them.
# Optionally point somewhere else:
# RESUME_GUIDELINES_PATH=/absolute/path/to/guidelines.md

# --- Rate limiting & AI spend caps (usage limits) ---
# There is no login: anyone can start a resume, so anyone can spend the Azure
# budget. Two independent controls bound that.
#
#   1. REQUEST LIMITS (per user, per hour; per IP for creating a resume) are CODE
#      constants in lib/rate-limit/policy.ts — each one is documented next to the
#      reasoning for its number and covered by tests. Change them there, not here.
#   2. SPEND CAPS (below) are money, so they are environment config: the right
#      ceiling depends on your Azure agreement and your traffic, and raising one
#      must not need a code change.
#
# !! ENFORCEMENT REQUIRES SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. The counters live in Postgres
# !! and are executable only by the service role, because the anon key ships to
# !! browsers — anything anon can call, an attacker can call with any key it likes.
# !! Without that variable the app still runs, logs a loud error, and enforces
# !! NOTHING. Apply supabase/migrations/0009_usage_limits.sql first.
#
# A worst-case resume — five experiences, every answer at its char limit, all three
# improvement rounds, plus the proofread — estimates at roughly $0.65-0.80 on
# gpt-5.3-codex rates (wc-tmp/worstcase.test.ts is the harness). A profile's FIRST
# generation is never refused by the per-resume or per-user cap, so these can be
# tight without withholding the actual product.
#
# Per resume, lifetime (default 1).
# AI_SPEND_CAP_PROFILE_USD=1
# Per user across all their resumes, lifetime (default 2).
# AI_SPEND_CAP_USER_USD=2
# Everyone, per UTC calendar day (default 50). The only ceiling that sees a flood
# of fresh guest identities, so it is the one that stops a distributed abuse run.
# Raising it is a bill decision: ~$50/day is roughly 150-250 finished resumes.
# AI_SPEND_CAP_DAILY_USD=50
#
# Local-development escape hatch. `off` skips every counter and cap. NEVER set this
# in a deployed environment.
# USAGE_LIMITS=off

# --- Talent directory ---
# The directory REQUIRES SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY (above). Unlike the rate
# limiters, it fails CLOSED without it: the public read functions in
# supabase/migrations/0010_talent_directory.sql are granted to service_role only,
# and contact records cannot be written at all. Apply that migration before
# offering the feature. That migration also needs the `unaccent` extension, which
# it creates itself — it is what makes searching "reposteria" find "Repostería".
#
# There is no talent-specific variable to set. Listing lifetime (90 days) and the
# request limits are CODE constants, for the same reason every other limit is —
# see lib/repositories/talent-store.ts and lib/rate-limit/policy.ts.

